    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/marthanda-varma"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/marthanda-varma.jpg" alt="Marthanda Varma" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/marthanda-varma">Marthanda Varma</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anizham_Thirunal_Marthanda_Varma.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Travancore takes its name from Thiruvithamcode, in the present Kanyakumari district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-nadu">Tamil Nadu</a>, contracted to Thiruvankode and anglicised by the English. The land at the tip of the subcontinent had belonged to the Ay dynasty, the earliest rulers of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerala">Kerala</a>, whose capital lay first at Aykudi and from the late 8th century at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kollam">Kollam</a>, where the Persian merchant Sulaiman al-Tajir found an extensive trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in the 9th century. As the Ays declined, Venad became the southernmost principality of the Second Chera Kingdom; a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Chola</a> invasion destroyed Kollam in 1096, and when the Chera capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahodayapuram">Mahodayapuram</a> fell too, the last Chera king Rama Varma Kulasekara moved his seat to Kollam and was probably the founder of the Venad royal house, whose rulers kept the title Kulasekara. In the later 12th century the Thrippappur and Chirava branches of the Ay line merged into the Venad family.</p><p>The Chera empire had dissolved by about 1100 and the region split into many small kingdoms until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marthanda-varma">Marthanda Varma</a>, king of Venad from 1729, unified them by brutal methods; in his reign Thiruvithamkoor became the official name. He defeated a union of feudal lords, established internal peace and in 29 years of campaigns extended Venad from Kanyakumari to the borders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kochi">Kochi</a>, with his prime minister Ramayyan Dalawa (1737–1756) at his side. The capital had moved in 1601 from Thiruvithamcode to Padmanabhapuram near Kalkulam, where it remained until 1795.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/swathi-thirunal"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/swathi-thirunal.jpg" alt="Swathi Thirunal" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/swathi-thirunal">Swathi Thirunal</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Swathi_Thirunal_of_Travancore.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Stephen Crening, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Travancore lay in three climatic belts: the rugged, cool eastern highlands, the rolling central midlands and the coastal lowlands of the west. By the 1941 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> the state had 6,070,018 people. Unlike the rest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> it divided the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rupee">rupee</a> into its own units: Cash and Chuckram coins were struck in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>, Fanam and Rupee coins in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>. The rulers spent only a small part of the revenue on themselves, but a famine in 1943 cost about 90,000 lives.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">Caste</a> was enforced more rigorously in Travancore than in much of India until the mid-1800s, the government turning the caste order into a religious institution; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nair">Nairs</a> and Tamil <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmins</a> alone dominated the bureaucracy into the early 20th century, and women of the backward castes were forbidden to wear an upper cloth, the cause of the 19th-century Upper Cloth Revolt. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swami-vivekananda">Swami Vivekananda</a> called the state "The Lunatic Asylum in India" for its discrimination, and the Vaikom Satyagraha exposed it. The Ayyavazhi sect of Ayya Vaikundar, whom <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swathi-thirunal">Swathi Thirunal</a> imprisoned and then freed, worked as a reform movement among the downtrodden, and the reform movements of the late 19th century gave Travancore the highest male literacy rate in India. Among the forward castes women held relatively high status, daughters inheriting property and holding the right to divorce and remarry, until laws of the regent Sethu Lakshmi Bayi in 1925 established patriarchy.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/tipu-sultan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tipu-sultan.jpg" alt="Tipu Sultan" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/tipu-sultan">Tipu Sultan</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:TipuSultan1790.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Anonymous artist from Mysore, India., Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The making of Travancore was military. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/travancore-dutch-war">Travancore–Dutch War</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marthanda-varma">Marthanda Varma</a>'s forces defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a>, allied to the small kingdoms he was absorbing, at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-colachel">Battle of Colachel</a> in 1741, a victory counted the first in which an organised Asian power overcame European military technology and tactics. The captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> captain Eustachius De Lannoy defected, became captain of the royal bodyguard and later Senior Admiral, and modernised the army with firearms and artillery. At Purakkad in 1755 he defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zamorin">Zamorin</a> of Kozhikode, making Travancore the dominant state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerala">Kerala</a>.</p><p>Under Dharma Raja, who refused to surrender <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> refugees from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysore">Mysore</a>'s occupation of Malabar, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tipu-sultan">Tipu Sultan</a>'s army entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kochi">Cochin</a> from Coimbatore in November 1789 and on 28 December attacked the Nedunkotta and was defeated at the Battle of Nedumkotta. Travancore often fought beside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">English East India Company</a>, but the alliance ended its own army. After a mutiny of 1805 against the dalawa Velu Thampi most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nair">Nair</a> battalions were disbanded, and when Velu Thampi and Paliath Achan of Cochin declared war on the Company in 1809, Company troops beat Paliath Achan at Cochin on 27 February and Velu Thampi's men near Nagercoil and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kollam">Kollam</a>; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharaja">Maharajah</a> sided with the British, Velu Thampi took to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> war and killed himself to avoid capture, and almost all remaining forces were disbanded, the Company undertaking the state's defence.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/lord-dalhousie"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lord-dalhousie.jpg" alt="Lord Dalhousie" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/lord-dalhousie">Lord Dalhousie</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dalhousie.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">George Richmond, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The king ruled through a Dewan, assisted by the Neetezhutthu Pillay (secretary), the Rayasom Pillay (under-secretary), Rayasoms (clerks) and Kanakku Pillamars (accountants); districts were run by Sarvadhikaris, and the Valia Sarvahi dealt with neighbouring states and Europeans and signed treaties. In 1856 the state was divided into three divisions, Northern Travancore (Cottayam), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kollam">Quilon</a> and Southern Travancore (Padmanabhapuram), each under a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/divan">Divan</a> Peishkar ranking with a British District Collector; by 1911 there were five, Padmanabhapuram and Devikulam largely Tamil-speaking, the rest Malayalam-speaking. Queens and regents ruled in their turn, Gowri Lakshmi Bayi, Gowri Parvati Bayi and Sethu Lakshmi Bayi.</p><p>Religion and kingship were bound together. On 3 January 1750 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marthanda-varma">Marthanda Varma</a> dedicated the state to Padmanabha, and the kings ruled as his servants; they were conditionally promoted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kshatriya">Kshatriya</a> status through sixteen mahādānams, until in 1848 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lord-dalhousie">Lord Dalhousie</a> warned Uttram Tirunal that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Madras</a> would take over the administration if the costly practice did not end. From 1788 Travancore accepted British dominance by treaty, entering a subsidiary alliance in 1795 and losing its political independence under the revised treaty of 1805. The dalawas and dewans grew powerful from Velu Thampi (1799–1809), and after 1811 British officers such as Colonel John Munro held the office.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/thiruvananthapuram"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thiruvananthapuram.jpg" alt="Thiruvananthapuram" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/thiruvananthapuram">Thiruvananthapuram</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Padmanabhaswamy_Temple_Thiruvananthapuram.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Shishirdasika, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Travancore was the most powerful state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerala">Kerala</a> from the 1750s, and its rulers, who spent only a small portion of the revenue on themselves, were unusually popular with their subjects. Dharma Raja's reign (1758–1798), assisted by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diwan">Diwan</a> Raja Kesavadas, is counted a golden age of social development, and the reform movements of the 19th century made the kingdom the region with the highest male literacy rate in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Under Chithira Thirunal the state built heavy industry, twenty public undertakings on local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">rubber</a>, ceramics and minerals that still form a majority of Kerala's premier industries, launched the first public transport route from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thiruvananthapuram">Thiruvananthapuram</a> to Mavelikkara and the first telecommunication line, appointed Dr G. H. Cousins the first Art Advisor to a government, and patronised musicians, dancers and Vedic scholars.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-entry-proclamation">Temple Entry Proclamation</a> of 12 November 1936 opened all the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> temples of Travancore to the backward communities and drew praise from across India, notably from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Mahatma Gandhi</a>. The state's politics also fed the language movements of independent India: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamils">Tamils</a> in the southern taluks founded the All Travancore Tamilian Congress at Nagercoil on 16 December 1945, renamed the Travancore <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-nadu">Tamil Nadu</a> Congress in 1946, which under A. Nesamony won 14 seats in the first assembly; the Fazal Ali Commission's report of 1955 gave the four southern taluks to Tamil Nadu as Kanyakumari district on 1 November 1956.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/c-p-ramaswami-iyer"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/c-p-ramaswami-iyer.jpg" alt="C. P. Ramaswami Iyer" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/c-p-ramaswami-iyer">C. P. Ramaswami Iyer</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:CPRamaswami_Aiyar_1939.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The Hindu, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Travancore's independence had been fading since 1788. Balarama Varma, who succeeded in 1798 at sixteen, was the weakest ruler of the dynasty, and the prime ministers took control; Velu Thampi's rebellion of 1809 left the Company responsible for the state's defence and British officers as dewans. The end came with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-independence">Indian independence</a>. Chithira Thirunal's prime minister, Sir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/c-p-ramaswami-iyer">C. P. Ramaswami Iyer</a>, was hated by the communists; a rising at Punnapra-Vayalar in 1946 set up its own government until the Travancore Army and Navy put it down, and in June 1947 Iyer announced that Travancore would remain an independent country rather than join the Indian Union.</p><p>An attempt on Iyer's life followed, and he resigned and left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Madras</a>, succeeded by P. G. N. Unnithan. Witnesses and historians hold that the riots did not sway the ruler; after negotiations with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/v-p-menon">V. P. Menon</a>, Chithira Thirunal agreed to accede to the Indian Union on 12 August 1947. On 1 July 1949 the kingdom was forced to merge with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kochi">Cochin</a> as the short-lived state of Travancore–Kochi, and on 1 November 1956 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerala">Kerala</a> came into being under a governor, its southern Tamil taluks going to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madras-state">Madras State</a>. The twenty-sixth amendment of 31 July 1971 stripped the former king of his remaining powers and privy purse. Chithira Thirunal died on 20 July 1991 after 67 years as ruler and titular <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharaja">Maharaja</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/government-of-india">Government of India</a> issued a stamp on 6 November 1991 commemorating his reforms.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Travancore was the princely state built from Venad by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marthanda-varma">Marthanda Varma</a> after 1729, which defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> at Colachel, dedicated itself to the deity Padmanabha, passed under British dominance by the treaties of 1788–1805, and in its last decades opened its temples to all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">castes</a> and built modern industry before merging into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in 1949.</em></p>

